  "annotations": [
    {
      "id": "n-capture",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -150,
      "w": 760,
      "page": "page-1",
      "text": "CAPTURE\nEvery capture starts with a command. Row 1 is discovery and the moment of capture. Row 2 is the three ways it does not go cleanly: a question left waiting, text typed without a command, and the shared model limit."
    },
    {
      "id": "n-cmdcount",
      "x": 3120,
      "y": -150,
      "w": 760,
      "page": "page-1",
      "text": "Only four commands exist in epic 001, because tasks are the only record type. The filter artboard shows /add matching one command, not six. The palette is built to group as the list grows."
    },
    {
      "id": "n-pending",
      "x": 1560,
      "y": 910,
      "w": 760,
      "page": "page-1",
      "text": "FR-36 to FR-38. The question sits in the transcript and never blocks. /tasks ran underneath it and worked. Nothing is saved until the question is answered, and the answer binds to its own capture."
    },
    {
      "id": "n-records",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -150,
      "w": 900,
      "page": "page-2",
      "text": "RECORDS\nOne type filter, All and Tasks, because tasks are the only record type in this epic. Detail shows the origin and what you actually typed, so a record can always be traced back to the command that made it."
    },
    {
      "id": "n-states",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -150,
      "w": 900,
      "page": "page-3",
      "text": "Timezone is the only setting in this epic. Jarvis has one account per user, so the no-permission state can only mean a session that ended."
    },
    {
      "id": "n-dir",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -170,
      "w": 900,
      "page": "page-4",
      "text": "Two directions not taken, kept low-fi so they read as alternatives rather than half-finished screens. The built direction is warm paper, IBM Plex, one blue for action and one amber for commands. Say the word if either of these is closer."
    },
    {
      "id": "n-mobile",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -190,
      "w": 1020,
      "page": "page-5",
      "text": "MOBILE\nSame product, same commands, 390x844. The rail becomes a bottom bar, the records table becomes stacked rows, and the three-up field card stacks.\n\nNo fake status bar and no fake keyboard are drawn. On MobileCommands the empty band under the input is exactly where the real keyboard renders."
    },
    {
      "id": "n-mobiletouch",
      "x": 1530,
      "y": -190,
      "w": 1020,
      "page": "page-5",
      "text": "Every control is at least 44px tall. The command list is tap-first here rather than arrow-key first, so the highlighted row is a default rather than a cursor."
    },
    {
      "id": "n-pwa",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -220,
      "w": 1020,
      "page": "page-6",
      "text": "INSTALLED APP\nThree surfaces a plain web app does not have: the invitation to install, being offline, and a new version waiting.\n\nOffline reads but does not capture. Capture needs a model call, so the input says so plainly instead of queueing work it cannot finish. That keeps the PRD's out-of-scope line on offline capture intact."
    },
    {
      "id": "n-pwapush",
      "x": 1530,
      "y": -220,
      "w": 1440,
      "page": "page-6",
      "text": "No push notification design here on purpose. The approved decision is in-app and email, no push. Installing makes web push cheap to add, which is worth reopening at epic 002 with reminders, as a decision rather than a side effect."
    },
    {
      "id": "n-dark",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -190,
      "w": 1020,
      "page": "page-7",
      "text": "DARK THEME\nNot the light palette inverted. Warm near-black rather than blue-grey, because the light theme is warm paper and both have to read as one product. Accents lighten so they stay legible; the token sheet gives every pair.\n\nThe toggle itself is on the Theme toggle page."
    },
    {
      "id": "n-names",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -220,
      "w": 1200,
      "page": "page-8",
      "text": "NAME, ROUND ONE\nSuperseded. Slash was chosen on 9 September and then found to collide with existing products, so the round was reopened.\n\nKept as the record of what was weighed. The lesson is on the next page: every candidate here was a common dictionary word, and common words are exactly what you cannot own."
    },
    {
      "id": "n-theme",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -200,
      "w": 1200,
      "page": "page-9",
      "text": "THEME TOGGLE\nThree choices, not two: System, Light, Dark. System is the default and follows the device through the day.\n\nThe two preview tiles are the point. A segmented control alone makes you switch the whole app to find out what you are choosing.\n\nThis needs proposed FR-42 approved. Without it the app follows the system and there is nothing to toggle."
    },
    {
      "id": "n-names2",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -230,
      "w": 1300,
      "page": "page-10",
      "text": "NAME, ROUND TWO\nRound one collided because all four were common English words. This round is built for ownability instead: two near-forgotten words with exact meanings, one mosaic term, one Roman root.\n\nEach card carries a collision risk read as well as a case and a cost.\n\nMy pick is Cairn: the plainest to say and spell, the easiest mark to draw, and the metaphor is the product.\n\nI cannot check domains or trademarks from here. Every risk note is reasoning from general knowledge and needs a real search before you commit."
    },
    {
      "id": "n-names3",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -230,
      "w": 1300,
      "page": "page-11",
      "text": "NAME, ROUND THREE\nClosed. Slashit was chosen on 9 September. These fourteen candidates stay as the record of what was weighed across three rounds.\n\nThe logo built from that choice is on the Logo page."
    },
    {
      "id": "n-logo",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -230,
      "w": 1300,
      "page": "page-12",
      "text": "LOGO\nThe mark is the keystroke. Every record begins with a slash, so the logo is the first thing the user types rather than a picture of something else.\n\nRead the Wordmark sheet second, and read it before you buy a domain. Set as one lowercase word, slashit breaks at the wrong place for a lot of readers. Three of the four treatments fix it; slash.it is the one I would ship.\n\nConstruction, lockups, misuse and application follow."
    }
  ],
  "launch": {
    "view": "canvas",
    "page": "page-12"
  }
}